  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Higher harmonics exceed the audio sampling rate, causing them to fold back to lower frequencies and effectively become subharmonics. This is also known as aliasing; an optical example is the [wagon-wheel effect](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wagon-wheel_effect), which you have probably seen in real life:


![](/attachments/image-88c.png)


%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Higher harmonics exceed the audio sampling rate, causing them to fold back to lower frequencies and effectively become subharmonics. This is also known as aliasing; an optical example is the [wagon-wheel effect](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wagon-wheel_effect), which you have probably seen in real life:


![](/attachments/image-88c.png)

