0.42 C-59.72 42.44, -58.12 44.46, -53.46 50.36 M-53.46 50.36 C-59.14 51.71, -64.82 53.06, -68.05 53.82 M-53.46 50.36 C-57.11 51.23, -60.76 52.09, -68.05 53.82 M-68.05 53.82 C-68.05 53.82, -68.05 53.82, -68.05 53.82 M-68.05 53.82 C-68.05 53.82, -68.05 53.82, -68.05 53.82" stroke="#1971c2" stroke-width="2" fill="none"></path></g></g><mask></mask><g stroke-opacity="0.6" fill-opacity="0.6" transform="translate(483.9815873406314 15.665391917496535) rotate(0 5.2299957275390625 12.5)"><text x="0" y="0" font-family="Virgil, Segoe UI Emoji" font-size="20px" fill="#1971c2" text-anchor="start" style="white-space: pre;" direction="ltr" dominant-baseline="text-before-edge">v</text></g><g stroke-opacity="0.6" fill-opacity="0.6" transform="translate(490.4958580816249 96.96281053391687) rotate(0 37.179969787597656 12.5)"><text x="0" y="0" font-family="Virgil, Segoe UI Emoji" font-size="20px" fill="#1971c2" text-anchor="start" style="white-space: pre;" direction="ltr" dominant-baseline="text-before-edge">v after</text></g></svg></div>

In principle, if either `x-dist` or `y-dist` becomes negative, then we have a collision. No matter which direction the ball comes from, we __simply flip__ the x-component of its velocity.


%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
If[
    Abs[ball[[2,1]] - paddles[[i,1]]] < $ballRadius + $paddles[[1]]/2 &&
    Abs[ball[[2,2]] - paddles[[i,2]]] < $ballRadius + $paddles[[2]]/2,
      (* reflect x-velocity *)
      ball[[{1,2}, 1]] = ball[[{2,1}, 1]];
      SFXPaddle // EmitSound;
]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
### Momentum transfer
__Timecode: 1:43:44__


In the original Pong game for Atari, the paddle's speed before a collision matters. This allows you to increase the ball's vertical speed by partially transferring the paddle's momentum to it. This not only makes the game more fun, but also adds a strategic element: you can make the reflection angle sharper, making the ball harder for the opponent to catch, or, conversely, slow it down by hitting it while moving in the opposite direction.

In an ideal *frictionless* world of spheres, this would not happen. But with friction $\\eta$ between the ball and a paddle, some of the paddle's momentum is transferred into the ball's rotational motion, while some contributes to the y-velocity of its center of mass — which is what we need. Simulating rotational dynamics (essentially a rigid-body simulation) would be overkill for a simple Pong game, though we might consider it in another stream. Therefore, we neglect the rotational degree of freedom and approximate the ball's physics with

!![1:]
Then we can add this modifier to the y-component of the ball's velocity after impact:

