troke-opacity="0.6" fill-opacity="0.6" transform="translate(387.8325297343524 90.08135562495335) rotate(0 -2.984375 2.861328125)"><path d="M0 0 C-0.99 0.95, -4.97 4.77, -5.97 5.72 M0 0 C-0.99 0.95, -4.97 4.77, -5.97 5.72" stroke="#1e1e1e" stroke-width="2" fill="none"></path></g></g><mask></mask><g stroke-linecap="round"><g stroke-opacity="0.6" fill-opacity="0.6" transform="translate(394.2016703593524 91.38213687495335) rotate(0 -3.0625 9.7109375)"><path d="M0 0 C-1.02 3.24, -5.1 16.18, -6.13 19.42 M0 0 C-1.02 3.24, -5.1 16.18, -6.13 19.42" stroke="#1e1e1e" stroke-width="2" fill="none"></path></g></g><mask></mask><g stroke-linecap="round"><g stroke-opacity="0.6" fill-opacity="0.6" transform="translate(399.1196391093524 104.70049624995335) rotate(0 1.24609375 12.548828125)"><path d="M0 0 C0.42 4.18, 2.08 20.91, 2.49 25.1 M0 0 C0.42 4.18, 2.08 20.91, 2.49 25.1" stroke="#1e1e1e" stroke-width="2" fill="none"></path></g></g><mask></mask><g stroke-linecap="round"><g stroke-opacity="0.6" fill-opacity="0.6" transform="translate(400.1782328593524 80.05205874995335) rotate(0 -0.390625 -4.92578125)"><path d="M0 0 C-0.13 -1.64, -0.65 -8.21, -0.78 -9.85 M0 0 C-0.13 -1.64, -0.65 -8.21, -0.78 -9.85" stroke="#1e1e1e" stroke-width="2" fill="none"></path></g></g><mask></mask></svg></div>

Then we can add this modifier to the y-component of the ball's velocity after impact:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\[Eta] (paddles[[1,2]]-prevPaddles[[1,2]]);

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
To rewrite this in terms of the Verlet method, we add it to the difference between the current and previous positions. To avoid choppy motion, we can add this difference to the previous coordinate.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
To rewrite this in terms of the Verlet method, we add it to the difference between the current and previous positions. To avoid choppy motion, we can add this difference to the previous coordinate.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
ball[[1,2]] -= \[Eta] (paddles[[1,2]]-prevPaddles[[1,2]]);

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
### Second player

For our CPU opponent, we use a [P controller](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proportional_control):

$$
\\text{paddle}_{y} = (1-\\alpha)~\\text{paddle}_{y} + \\alpha ~ \text{ball}_{y}
$$

where $\\alpha$ determines how quickly our second player reacts.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
### Second player

For our CPU opponent, we use a [P controller](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proportional_control):

$$
\\text{paddle}_{y} = (1-\\alpha)~\\text{paddle}_{y} + \\alpha ~ \text{ball}_{y}
$$

where $\\alpha$ determines how quickly our second player reacts.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
### Scoring system
This is fairly straightforward: we add a point to the first or second player's score, depending on which wall the ball hits (left or right). Text is displayed using the `Text` primitive inside a `Graphics` expression.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
### Scoring system
This is fairly straightforward: we add a point to the first or second player's score, depending on which wall the ball hits (left or right). Text is displayed using the `Text` primitive inside a `Graphics` expression.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
